  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, a weak ridge remains over the Southeast as a \nbroad trough moves into the Midwest and Ohio Valley. At the surface, \na cold front will be over the Ohio Valley today. Mostly dry weather \nwill continue through this afternoon although a few stray showers \nwill be possible mainly over the upper Cumberland Plateau. This \nevening and into the overnight hours, showers will increase, mainly \nin the northern third of the region as a decaying cold front slowly \nmoves down from the north. Thunder chances are low with decreasing \ninstability. CAMs keep showers weak but reflects better coverage \nacross the region whereas the NBM keeps showers well to the north of \nKnoxville.  \n\nDaily rain chances are expected mainly in the afternoon and evening \nhours from Friday through the weekend. In the upper levels, a \nshortwave will be over the Lower Mississippi Valley. The weak \nsurface boundary will also be lingering over or near the region \nthrough the weekend. Deep layer moisture advection is likely with \nhigh pressure over the Atlantic bringing southerly surface winds and \na trough to the west bringing higher level moisture from the Gulf. \nShowers and storms will likely be scattered at times Friday through \nSaturday. NBM has the highest chances on Saturday afternoon.\n\nThe best chance for strong to severe storms will be Saturday with \ngood instability around 2000 J/kg. With weak shear and high freezing \nlevels the primary threat will be gusty winds. With good moisture \nadvection, Pwat values will be elevated around 2 inches on Saturday \nso heavy downpours will be likely at times that may cause some \nisolated flooding. Steering flow around 15 knots should help keep \nshowers and storms moving, so overall the flooding threat is low.\nFriday has a lower threat of impact weather with lower instability \nand lower Pwat values. By Sunday, the shortwave to the west is gone \nand a weak ridge builds into the Southeast. Therefore, showers and \nstorms will be more isolated on Sunday and perhaps more scattered in \nthe mountains.\n\nEarly next week, a ridge will be over the Southeast. Isolated to \nscattered showers and storms will be possible with the highest \nchances in and near the terrain. Afternoon highs will be above \nnormal throughout the forecast period with upper 80s expected near \nTri-Cities and lower 90s for Knoxville and Chattanooga areas, \ngenerally about 6 to 8 degrees above normal. Chattanooga is expected \nto exceed 90 degrees again today and tomorrow. If that happens, they \nwill break the record for consecutive days with a high temp of 90 or \ngreater. The current record is 38 days back in 1993. Saturday the \nforecast is 90 for Chattanooga but that may not happen if clouds and \nconvection occur earlier in the day.",
